## 5. Day 1: Preprocessing and Tokenization

### 5.1 Text preprocessing

Raw text is noisy. A computer treats "Football" and "football" as different words, so text is normalized first.

| Step | Effect | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Lowercase | All letters become small | Merges different spellings of one word |
| Remove HTML tags | Deletes `<b>` and similar | Tags carry no meaning |
| Remove links | Deletes `http...` and `www...` | Mostly noise |
| Keep letters only | Removes numbers and symbols | Smaller vocabulary |
| Remove stopwords | Deletes words such as *the, is, of* | They do not help separate topics |

> **Design choice:** one function (`clean_for_model`) is used in the notebook and in the API. If training and prediction used different cleaning, the model would see different kinds of text and make more mistakes.

### 5.2 Tokenization

Tokenization splits text into units that are later turned into numbers.

| Level | Example | Drawback |
|---|---|---|
| Word | `football` | Huge vocabulary, cannot handle unseen words |
| Character | `f o o t b a l l` | Very long sequences, little meaning per token |
| **Subword** | `foot` + `##ball` | Balanced: rare words are built from known pieces |

### 5.3 Byte Pair Encoding (BPE)

BPE merges the **most frequent** neighbouring pair of symbols, again and again.

1. Write each word as letters plus an end marker: `l o w </w>`
2. Count all neighbouring pairs in the training text.
3. Merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol (`l + o` becomes `lo`).
4. Repeat for a fixed number of merges (300 here).

New text is tokenized by applying the learned merges in order. Symbols not in the vocabulary become `<unk>`.

### 5.4 WordPiece

WordPiece (used by BERT) merges the pair with the best **score**:

```
score(A, B) = frequency(AB) / ( frequency(A) x frequency(B) )
```

This favours pairs whose parts rarely appear apart. Pieces inside a word start with `##`. Tokenizing uses **greedy longest-match-first**; if nothing matches, the word becomes `[UNK]`.

| Aspect | BPE | WordPiece |
|---|---|---|
| Merge rule | Most frequent pair | Highest score above |
| Marker | `</w>` ends a word | `##` marks inner pieces |
| Tokenizing | Apply merges in order | Longest match from the left |
| Unknown | `<unk>` | `[UNK]` for the whole word |
| Used by | GPT models | BERT |

Both were implemented from scratch for learning, then compared on **vocabulary size**, **token count** and **unknown-word handling**.

