## 7. Day 3: Classification and LLMs

### 7.1 TF-IDF + Logistic Regression

A supervised pipeline: TF-IDF turns text into numbers, then **Logistic Regression** learns which words point to which class. For several classes it uses the **softmax** function to convert scores into probabilities that sum to 1; the highest one is the prediction. The pipeline is saved as a pickle file (`tfidf_logreg.pkl`) so it can be loaded without retraining.

### 7.2 LLM classification (Groq)

A Large Language Model is asked to classify the article through a **prompt**. No training is needed.

| Mode | Prompt contains |
|---|---|
| **Zero-shot** | Task description and allowed labels only |
| **Few-shot** | The same, plus one solved example per class (300 characters each) |

| Setting | Value | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Model | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | Free on Groq; `llama-3.1-8b-instant` was retired |
| `temperature` | 0 | Stable, repeatable answers |
| `max_tokens` | 300 | The model "thinks" first and thinking uses tokens |
| `reasoning_effort` | low | Shorter thinking, faster and cheaper |
| Article length | cut to 1,500 characters | Saves tokens and rate limit |
| Parsing | match reply to a known label, else `unknown` | Protects against odd replies |
| API key | `.env` file | Secrets never go in code or Git |

The response includes `raw_answer`, the exact LLM text before it is matched to a label, which helps to debug wrong or `unknown` results.

### 7.3 Evaluation metrics

| Metric | Formula | Question |
|---|---|---|
| Accuracy | correct / all | How often is it right overall? |
| Precision | TP / (TP + FP) | When it predicts a class, is it correct? |
| Recall | TP / (TP + FN) | Of all real items of a class, how many were found? |
| F1 | 2PR / (P + R) | One balanced number |

Precision, recall and F1 are computed per class and combined with a **weighted average** (larger classes count more). A **confusion matrix** shows which classes get mixed up.

